---
import ArticleLayout from "@/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import CodeWindow from "@/components/CodeWindow.astro";
import booksCode from "../../examples/playwright-books.mjs?raw";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Run Playwright without Chromium using Mimic"
  eyebrow="JavaScript extraction"
  description="Connect Playwright to Mimic over CDP, extract JSON from Books to Scrape, and understand the limits of automation without Chromium."
>
  <p>
    Mimic runs website JavaScript in V8 and exposes browser state over the
    Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP), without embedding Chromium. It is a
    source-available public beta for nonvisual automation and web scraping.
    Playwright remains the client; Mimic is the runtime you start separately.
  </p>
  <h2>Start Mimic and install the client</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={`${base}download/`}>Download Mimic</a> for Windows or Linux amd64, extract
    the archive and start the executable. The packaged runtime needs no Chrome, Go
    or Rust. Keep the unauthenticated CDP endpoint bound to loopback.
  </p>
  <CodeWindow
    title="Windows / Linux"
    lang="bash"
    code={`# Windows
./mimic.exe -listen 127.0.0.1:9222

# Linux
./mimic -listen 127.0.0.1:9222

# In a separate terminal, in your Node.js project
npm install playwright-core@1.63.0`}
  />
  <p>
    There is no Playwright browser download in this workflow. Use{" "}
    <code>chromium.connectOverCDP()</code> to attach to Mimic's endpoint.
    <code>chromium.launch()</code> would launch a separate Chromium executable.
  </p>
  <h2>Extract book titles and URLs as JSON</h2>
  <p>
    Save this as <code>playwright-books.mjs</code> and run{" "}
    <code>node playwright-books.mjs</code>. <a
      href="https://books.toscrape.com/">Books to Scrape</a
    >{" "}
    is a public scraping sandbox. Its catalog is static: this example validates navigation,
    DOM extraction and a JavaScript evaluation, not compatibility with an arbitrary
    JavaScript application.
  </p>
  <CodeWindow title="playwright-books.mjs" code={booksCode} />
  <p>
    The expected output is an <code>All products</code> heading and 20 book records
    with titles and absolute URLs. HTTP failures or changed page content produce
    an error. The script closes its Page and disconnects; stop the Mimic process
    separately when finished.
  </p>
  <p>
    This example was checked with the Mimic v0.1.9 Windows release and{" "}
    <code>playwright-core 1.63.0</code>. That is a checkpoint for this script,
    not a guarantee for other sites or all client methods.
  </p>
  <h2>What CDP compatibility means here</h2>
  <p>
    <a
      href="https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-browsertype#browser-type-connect-over-cdp"
      >Playwright documents CDP attachment</a
    >{" "}
    as lower fidelity than its native protocol connection. Mimic implements a subset
    of the browser behavior expected by that CDP client.
  </p>
  <p>
    Using the same client does not make Mimic a drop-in replacement for every
    Playwright workflow. Mimic has partial CDP and Web API compatibility,
    targets observable Chrome 152 behavior and supports independent Pages and
    Contexts. A protocol schema entry alone is not evidence that a command is
    implemented.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check the <a
      href="https://github.com/mimic-browser/runtime/blob/main/docs/cdp-compatibility.md"
      >CDP compatibility notes</a
    >{" "}
    and <a
      href="https://github.com/mimic-browser/runtime/blob/main/docs/cdp-coverage-generated.md"
      >semantic support matrix</a
    >{" "}
    for your commands. Mimic can return an approximate PNG through{" "}
    <code>Page.captureScreenshot</code>, but visual accuracy is not guaranteed. Video and
    rendered PDFs remain unsupported, and modeled geometry does not establish
    visual fidelity.
  </p>
  <h2>Move a real workload in small steps</h2>
  <p>
    First compare extracted fields, navigation and session behavior against your
    existing Chrome workflow. Check client-version compatibility, unsupported
    commands and target-site errors before adding concurrency. Keep Chromium
    available for jobs that need rendering or unsupported browser behavior.
  </p>
  <p>
    Continue with <a href={`${base}mimic-vs-chromium/`}
      >Mimic versus Chromium for repeated extraction</a
    >, the <a href={`${base}docs/compatibility/`}
      >setup and compatibility guide</a
    >, or <a href={`${base}examples/`}>Puppeteer, Python and raw CDP examples</a
    >.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
